Subject: Re: 9FX Vision 330 (PCI) == DEC PowerStorm 3D10?  

Tom,

Yes. The Powerstorm 3D10 is a....

The PowerStorm 3D10 appears to be a marketing name for the S3 Trio64 (2MB)
Graphics Option. This card is referenced in the PowerStorm marketing literature
as the low-end graphics controller in the PowerStorm Series. Although, the
order number referenced in the literature found to date references the PB2GA-JA
S3 Trio64 2MB PCI Graphics Controller. 

PB2GA-JA S3 Trio

                Hardware & General Option Information

Option Name: S3 Trio64 - 9FX Vision 330

Description: Color 2D, 8 plane Accelerator 256 Colors, 64-bit PCI Card, Vendor= 
#9

Console Device Name: Trio64

Switches / Jumpers: No Switch or Jumper Settings 

Amount of Off Screen Memory: 2MB Video Memory

Cabling: 

Uses 15-pin Sub-D to 5 BNC Lead Cable BC13L-10.
Seperate Horizontal and Vertical Synch at TTL levels.

Part Number: 30-46142-01

Available Documentation: 

9FX Vision 330 (PCI) Graphics option - Owern's Guide 
Part Number: EK-V330G-OG.

Comments: 

Single PCI Slot Short Form Factor.

Supports several resolutions and refresh rates configured by the
Operating System Xserver parameters. (See Operating System Comments)

This card is not supported in Multihead configurations with other S3Trio64
or ATIMach64 graphics cards under OpenVMS or Digital Unix for the following 
reason.(Reference ALPHASTATION Note #1453.0)

    The S3Trio64 card is a warmed over ISA design by S3.  It's operation
    requires the use of a number of VGA space registers (the cursor
    for instance) and it has no inherent ability to enable/disable the
    card from responding to those addresses (aside from PCI config space
    enable/disable).  What this essentially means is that a vanilla
    S3 TRio64 card (which is what Diamond and #9 sell) cannot be used for
    general multi-head.
        
It is expected that this card in combination with a ZLXP-En or PowerStorm card
will work, but this has yet to be qualified by Workstation Engineering (July 
1996).


